complete_reproduction_plan:
  paper_info:
    title: "Batch and Match: Score-Based Black-Box Variational Inference"
    core_contribution: >
      Reproduce the score-based divergence for Gaussian variational families, the
      Batch-and-Match (BaM) proximal update solving a quadratic matrix equation,
      its convergence proof behavior on Gaussian targets, and the empirical
      comparisons with ADVI/Score/Fisher/GSM on Gaussian, sinh-arcsinh,
      hierarchical posterior, and CIFAR-10 deep generative targets.

  # SECTION 1: File Structure Design
  file_structure: |
    project_root/
      README.md                         # LAST: usage, experiment commands, references
      requirements.txt                  # LAST: pinned dependencies
      src/bam/
        __init__.py
        quadratic_solver.py             # Core full/low-rank quadratic matrix solver
        batch_stats.py                  # Batch sample/score statistics
        algorithm.py                    # BaM batch-and-match Algorithm 1
        match_update.py                 # Closed-form mean/covariance MATCH updates
        divergences.py                  # Score-based divergence, KL, Fisher diagnostics
        baselines.py                    # ADVI, Score, Fisher, GSM algorithms
        targets.py                      # Gaussian, sinh-arcsinh target densities/scores
        posterior_targets.py            # posteriordb/BridgeStan posterior wrappers
        deep_generative.py              # CIFAR-10 VAE decoder/encoder and AVI helpers
        utils.py                        # JAX seeding, metrics, timing, plotting helpers
      experiments/
        run_gaussian_targets.py         # Section 5.1 Gaussian targets
        run_sinh_arcsinh.py             # Section 5.1 Non-Gaussian skew/tail targets
        run_posteriordb.py              # Section 5.2 Hierarchical Bayesian models
        run_cifar.py                    # Section 5.3 Deep generative model
        plot_results.py                 # Reproduce Figures 5.2-5.4, E.1-E.7
      tests/
        test_quadratic_solver.py
        test_match_update.py
        test_divergences.py
        test_batch_stats.py
        test_gaussian_convergence.py
        test_baselines.py

  # SECTION 2: Implementation Components
  implementation_components: |
    A. Score-based divergence and Gaussian specialization
       File: src/bam/divergences.py
       - General definition:
           D(q;p) = E_q[ ( grad log q - grad log p )^T
                   Gamma_q^{-1} ( grad log q - grad log p ) ]
         where Gamma_q = E_q[ (grad log q)(grad log q)^T ].
       - For q = N(nu, Psi):
           Gamma_q = Psi^{-1}
           D(q;p) = E_q[ (grad log p - grad log q)^T Psi
                   (grad log p - grad log q) ].
       - Empirical batch estimate used by BaM for candidate q=N(mu,Sigma):
           \hat D_{q_t}(q;p) = (1/B) sum_b
             || grad log q(z_b) - grad log p(z_b) ||_Sigma^2.
         Expand to:
           tr(Gamma Sigma) + tr(C Sigma^{-1})
             + ||mu - zbar - Sigma gbar||_{Sigma^{-1}}^2 + const.
       - Implement functions:
           score_divergence_gaussian(mu, Sigma, target_score, z_samples)
           kl_gaussian(mu0, Sigma0, mu1, Sigma1)
           fisher_divergence_estimate(...) for baseline.

    B. Batch statistics
       File: src/bam/batch_stats.py
       - Given z_1,...,z_B ~ q_t and target score g_b = grad_z log p(z_b):
           zbar  = (1/B) sum_b z_b
           gbar  = (1/B) sum_b g_b
           C     = (1/B) sum_b (z_b - zbar)(z_b - zbar)^T
           Gamma = (1/B) sum_b (g_b - gbar)(g_b - gbar)^T
       - Return all four quantities. Use JAX for differentiability of g if required.

    C. Quadratic matrix equation solver
       File: src/bam/quadratic_solver.py
       - Solve X U X + X = V with U,V symmetric positive semidefinite.
       - Full-rank O(D^3) solution:
           X = 2 V [ I + (I + 4 U V)^(1/2) ]^{-1}
         Use eigendecomposition or JAX scipy sqrtm/inv.
       - Low-rank O(K D^2 + K^3) solution when U = Q Q^T with Q in R^{DxK}:
           X = V - V^T Q [ 0.5 I + (Q^T V Q + 0.25 I)^(1/2) ]^{-2} Q^T V.
       - Utility to factor U = Q Q^T via eigendecomposition keeping positive
         eigenvalues; fall back to full solver if rank too large.
       - Validate symmetry and positive definiteness.

    D. BaM algorithm and MATCH update
       Files: src/bam/algorithm.py, src/bam/match_update.py
       Algorithm 1 / Appendix C:
         Input T, B, target log-density or score, lambda_t, mu0, Sigma0.
         For t=0,...,T-1:
           1. Sample z_1,...,z_B ~ N(mu_t, Sigma_t).
           2. Compute scores g_b = grad_z log p(z_b).
           3. Batch step: compute zbar, gbar, C, Gamma.
           4. Match covariance:
                U = lambda_t Gamma + lambda_t/(1+lambda_t) gbar gbar^T
                V = Sigma_t + lambda_t C
                    + lambda_t/(1+lambda_t)(mu_t - zbar)(mu_t - zbar)^T
                Sigma_{t+1} solves
                    Sigma U Sigma + Sigma = V
                using:
                    Sigma_{t+1} = 2 V [ I + (I + 4 U V)^(1/2) ]^{-1}
                or low-rank solver when U is low rank.
           5. Match mean:
                mu_{t+1} = lambda_t/(1+lambda_t) (zbar + Sigma_{t+1} gbar)
                           + 1/(1+lambda_t) mu_t.
       - Objective minimized:
           L_BaM(q) = \hat D_{q_t}(q;p) + (2/lambda_t) KL(q_t;q).
       - Learning rate schedules:
           Gaussian targets: lambda_t = B D constant.
           Non-Gaussian/posterior: lambda_t = B D / (t+1) decaying.
           Deep generative pilot: T=100 pilot to choose learning rate,
           then T=1000.
       - GSM limiting case: lambda -> infinity, B=1 gives
           U = g_t g_t^T,
           V = Sigma_t + (mu_t - z_t)(mu_t - z_t)^T,
           mu_{t+1} = Sigma_{t+1} g_t + z_t.

    E. Baseline algorithms
       File: src/bam/baselines.py
       - ADVI (Algorithm 2):
           Sample z_1,...,z_B ~ q_t.
           Negative ELBO:
             L_ELBO = -sum_b [ log p(z_b) - log q_t(z_b) ].
           Update variational parameters w_t=(mu_t,Sigma_t) with ADAM:
             w_{t+1} = ADAM(w_t, grad_w L_ELBO).
       - Score baseline:
           Use empirical score divergence in place of negative ELBO.
       - Fisher baseline:
           Use Fisher divergence:
             E_q[ || grad log q - grad log p ||^2 ].
       - GSM (Algorithm 3):
           For b=1..B:
             s_b = grad_z log p(z_b)
             epsilon_b = Sigma_t s_b - mu_t + z_b
             solve rho(1+rho) = s_b^T Sigma_t s_b + ((mu_t - z_b)^T s_b)^2
             delta_mu_b = 1/(1+rho) [ I
               - (mu_t - z_b)s_b^T / (1 + rho + (mu_t - z_b)^T s_b) ] epsilon_b
             tilde_mu_b = mu_t + delta_mu_b
             delta_Sigma_b = (mu_t - z_b)(mu_t - z_b)^T
               - (tilde_mu_b - z_b)(tilde_mu_b - z_b)^T
           mu_{t+1} = mu_t + (1/B)sum_b delta_mu_b
           Sigma_{t+1} = Sigma_t + (1/B)sum_b delta_Sigma_b.

    F. Target distributions
       File: src/bam/targets.py
       - Gaussian target:
           Sigma_* = A A^T with A random D x D matrix.
           mu_* generated or fixed; initialization mu0 ~ Uniform[0,0.1],
           Sigma0 = I.
       - Sinh-arcsinh normal:
           If y ~ N(mu,Sigma), then
             z = sinh((asinh(y) + s) / tau)
           with skew s and tail weight tau.
           Solve y(z) = sinh(tau * asinh(z) - s).
           Log density:
             log p_z(z) = log p_y(y(z)) + log |dy/dz|.
           Score computed by JAX autodiff through log p_z.
           Experiments use:
             skew cases: tau=1, s in {0.2, 1.0, 1.8}
             tail cases: s=0, tau in {0.1, 0.9, 1.7}.
       - Hierarchical posterior targets:
           File: src/bam/posterior_targets.py
           Load posteriordb models:
             ark (D=7)
             gp-pois-regr (D=13)
             eight-schools-centered (D=10)
           Use BridgeStan to evaluate unnormalized log posterior and
           gradient for target scores.
           Load HMC reference samples from posteriordb for mean/SD errors.

    G. Deep generative model / CIFAR-10
       File: src/bam/deep_generative.py
       - Latent z in R^256, observation x in R^3072.
       - Decoder:
           x | z ~ N(Omega(z, theta_hat), sigma^2 I), sigma^2=0.1.
         Omega is a neural network trained as VAE decoder.
       - Train encoder and decoder on CIFAR-10 to obtain theta_hat and
         amortized variational encoder for AVI baseline.
       - Posterior for a new observation x':
           p(z|x') proportional to p(z) p(x'|z,theta_hat).
       - Inference:
           BaM and ADVI use full-covariance Gaussian q(z|x').
           AVI uses factorized Gaussian encoder.
       - Evaluation:
           Reconstruction x'_hat = Omega(E[z|x'],theta_hat).
           MSE = mean ||x' - x'_hat||^2 over test data.
       - Experiments:
           BaM batch sizes B=10 and B=300; ADVI B=10; GSM.
           Pilot run T=100 to set learning rates for ADVI/BaM,
           final runs T=1000.
           Budget scenario: 3000 gradient evaluations; compare
           ADVI B=10,T=300 vs BaM B=300,T=10.

    H. Utilities
       File: src/bam/utils.py
       - JAX PRNG key management, wallclock timing, forward/reverse KL
         evaluation for Gaussian targets, relative mean and SD error metrics,
         plotting helpers.

  # SECTION 3: Validation & Evaluation
  validation_approach: |
    A. Unit tests
       - Quadratic solver:
           Verify X U X + X = V for random positive semidefinite U,V.
           Compare full-rank and low-rank solutions.
           Verify symmetry and positive definiteness.
       - Match update:
           Check covariance update solves the quadratic matrix equation.
           Check mean update matches closed form.
       - Divergence:
           Verify Gaussian-vs-Gaussian closed form:
             D(q;p)=tr[(I-Psi Sigma^{-1})^2]
                   +(nu-mu)^T Sigma^{-1} Psi Sigma^{-1}(nu-mu).
           Verify annealing, tilting, affine invariance properties.
       - Batch stats:
           Compare Monte Carlo batch means/covariances to known Gaussian
           moments in expectation.

    B. Theorem 1 convergence on Gaussian targets
       - Run BaM with very large batch size B on Gaussian target p=N(mu_*,Sigma_*).
       - Compute normalized mean error:
           eps_t = Sigma_*^{-1/2}(mu_t - mu_*)
         and normalized covariance error:
           Delta_t = Sigma_*^{-1/2} Sigma_t Sigma_*^{-1/2} - I.
       - Expected behavior:
           ||eps_t|| <= (1-delta)^t ||eps_0||
           ||Delta_t|| <= (1-delta)^t ||Delta_0||
                         + t (1-delta)^{t-1} ||eps_0||^2
         with beta = min(alpha,(1+lambda)/(1+lambda+||eps_0||^2)),
         delta = lambda beta/(1+lambda).
       - Test one-step convergence when lambda_0 is very large and B is very
         large; expect eps_1 and Delta_1 near zero.

    C. Section 5.1 Gaussian targets
       Script: experiments/run_gaussian_targets.py
       - Dimensions D in {4,16,64,128,256}, random Gaussian targets.
       - Initialization: mu0 ~ Uniform[0,0.1], Sigma0=I.
       - Algorithms: BaM, ADVI, Score, Fisher, GSM.
       - BaM learning rate: lambda_t = B D.
       - Metrics:
           Forward KL(q_t||p) and reverse KL(p||q_t).
           Number of gradient evaluations to reach specified KL threshold.
           Wallclock time per run.
       - Expected:
           BaM converges orders of magnitude faster than ADVI.
           Gradient-based methods ADVI/Score/Fisher have similar performance;
           Score is more learning-rate sensitive.
           GSM has marginal gains beyond B=2.

    D. Section 5.1 non-Gaussian targets
       Script: experiments/run_sinh_arcsinh.py
       - Skew cases: tau=1, s in {0.2,1.0,1.8}.
       - Tail cases: s=0, tau in {0.1,0.9,1.7}.
       - BaM uses decaying lr lambda_t = B D / (t+1).
       - Expected:
           BaM converges faster than ADVI.
           For large skew, BaM reaches higher forward KL but similar reverse KL.
           With s=1.8, GSM reverse KL diverges; Score may diverge.
           Tail-weight cases: all methods converge to similar reverse KL;
           BaM typically uses fewer gradient evaluations than ADVI.

    E. Section 5.2 hierarchical posteriors
       Script: experiments/run_posteriordb.py
       - Models: ark (D=7), gp-pois-regr (D=13), eight-schools-centered (D=10).
       - Algorithms: BaM, ADVI, GSM with B=8 and B=32.
       - BaM learning rate: lambda_t = B D / (t+1).
       - Metrics:
           Relative error of posterior mean estimates to HMC reference samples.
           Relative error of posterior SD estimates to HMC reference samples.
       - Expected:
           BaM outperforms ADVI.
           GSM may converge faster at B=8 but oscillates around the solution.
           BaM improves and stabilizes with B=32, whereas ADVI/GSM do not.
           In the hierarchical example, BaM may have larger relative SD error.

    F. Section 5.3 deep generative model
       Script: experiments/run_cifar.py
       - CIFAR-10 images flattened to R^3072, latent z in R^256.
       - Train VAE decoder with sigma^2=0.1.
       - Run BaM and ADVI full-covariance posterior inference; AVI baseline.
       - Use T=100 pilot runs to choose learning rates; T=1000 final runs.
       - Metrics:
           Reconstruction MSE using E[z|x'] fed through decoder.
           Wallclock time.
       - Expected:
           BaM poor for B=10 relative to D=256, competitive for B=300.
           With B=300 BaM converges an order of magnitude faster than
           ADVI and GSM.
           Under 3000 gradient evaluations: ADVI B=10,T=300 is competitive
           with BaM B=300,T=10; BaM gradients are parallelizable.
           BaM and ADVI eventually beat factorized AVI reconstruction error.

    G. Figure reproduction
       Script: experiments/plot_results.py
       - Plot forward/reverse KL curves for Figures 5.2 and E.2-E.3.
       - Plot posterior relative mean/SD errors for Figure 5.3.
       - Plot reconstruction images and MSE for Figure 5.4 and E.7.
       - Plot wallclock timings for Figure E.1.

  # SECTION 4: Environment & Dependencies
  environment_setup: |
    - Python 3.10 or 3.11.
    - Core numerical/ML:
        jax>=0.4.20
        jaxlib>=0.4.20
        numpy>=1.24
        scipy>=1.10
        matplotlib>=3.7
        pandas>=2.0
    - Probabilistic programming / posteriors:
        posteriordb
        bridgestan
        stanio
    - Tests:
        pytest>=7.0
    - Optional GPU:
        Any CUDA-compatible JAX installation if available; CPU is sufficient
        for low-dimensional examples but CIFAR-10 experiments benefit from GPU.
    - Hardware:
        Memory: >=16 GB RAM recommended for CIFAR-10 VAE and full-covariance
        operations in D=256 latent space.
        Disk: CIFAR-10 dataset and posteriordb downloaded cache.

  # SECTION 5: Implementation Strategy
  implementation_strategy: |
    Phase 1: Core numerical components
      1. Implement quadratic_solver.py with full and low-rank solvers.
      2. Add tests for solver accuracy, symmetry, positive definiteness,
         and agreement between solvers.
      3. Implement batch_stats.py and test against known Gaussian moments.

    Phase 2: Score-based divergence and BaM
      4. Implement divergences.py including Gaussian specialization and KL.
      5. Implement match_update.py and algorithm.py.
      6. Test:
         - On Gaussian targets with large B, check one-step near-convergence
           for large lambda.
         - Recover GSM update in B=1, lambda -> infinity limit.
         - Validate exponential convergence bounds from Theorem 1.

    Phase 3: Baselines
      7. Implement ADVI with ADAM and score/Fisher variants.
      8. Implement GSM per-sample update from Algorithm 3.
      9. Test on simple Gaussian targets where ADVI/GSM behavior is known.

    Phase 4: Target distributions and main experiments
      10. Implement Gaussian and sinh-arcsinh targets with JAX-autodiff scores.
      11. Reproduce Section 5.1 Gaussian and non-Gaussian experiments.
      12. Implement posteriordb/BridgeStan wrappers and reproduce Section 5.2.
      13. Implement CIFAR-10 VAE training/inference and reproduce Section 5.3.

    Phase 5: Plotting and final validation
      14. Implement plot_results.py for all main figures and appendices.
      15. Create README.md and requirements.txt after all APIs are stable.
      16. Run full test suite and experiment suite; confirm qualitative and
          quantitative trends match the paper within stochastic error.

    Missing-detail defaults:
      - If exact random seeds are absent, use fixed seeds across runs and
        report mean/standard error over the paper's 5-10 runs.
      - If exact neural architectures for CIFAR-10 VAE are not specified,
        use a standard convolutional encoder/decoder with dense latent layer
        of dimension 256 and constant decoder variance sigma^2=0.1.
      - If posteriordb sampling details are missing, load provided HMC
        reference draws and use the same target densities exposed by
        BridgeStan for score evaluation.
